      },
      "lineNbr": 473,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000032
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T01:09:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would have expected that instead of hardcoding the temp buffers all to be the same usage, you could request a particular usage - or even a whole buffer descriptor. And the buffer manager would try to find a suitable buffer for you. If the whole descriptor, we could support the `mappedAtCreation` case at [1] better.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 473,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 473,
        "endChar": 98
      },
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7cbbf5ab_55260a89",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 473,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T05:24:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think having buffer manager managing arbitrary usage could make it more complicated and have unnecessary overhead. We would have to have another layer of usage keying in the table, we would need another algorithm to find best fit usage, etc.\n\nCurrently there are only 3 usages needing temp buffer:\n1. For pure GPU purposes (copies, compute).\n  - Copies and compute don\u0027t necessarily need the same usage but hardcoding the aggregated usage for them could maximize the buffer reuses.\n  - We shouldn\u0027t reuse the buffers immediately after their commands has been submitted, because it would add inter command buffers\u0027 dependencies and prevent overlapping in GPU.\n2. uniform buffers.\n  - Need exact size in D3D11, and we don\u0027t need to wait until the buffers are done on GPU because D3D11\u0027s `WriteBuffer` can discard the old buffer\u0027s content and avoid command buffers\u0027 dependencies. This is particular useful opportunity for optimization because we don\u0027t need to allocate too many buffers.\n3. upload buffers.\n  - Need mappedAtCreation. I\u0027m not fully familiar with DynamicUploader class but maybe in future we could reuse the buffer manager class for allocating these buffers.",
      "parentUuid": "e6f611c3_e95ba80c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 473,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 473,
        "endChar": 98
      },
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "20c11045_0bf53c5c",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 2433,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000032
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T01:09:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "probably we should return a MutexProtected\u003cTempGPUBufferManager\u003e\u0026 to be safe for multiple encoding threads.\n\nThen, we should make it safer against re-entrancy since currently TempGPUBufferManager stores the Device and uses it to create buffers. That\u0027s a little scary because it\u0027s feasible that some workaround may need buffer creation to make another temp buffer. Then there\u0027s a deadlock.\n\nIt might make more sense to have the interface be:\n - Buffer TakeTemporaryBuffer(..., ExecutionSerial); // try to get a tmp buffer from the LRU cache. Update its serial.\n - void RecycleBuffer(Buffer, ExecutionSerial); // push a tmp buffer for future recycling.\n - void Trim(ExecutionSerial);  // trim the LRU cache\n \nThen, the LRU doesn\u0027t do any allocations. It only tracks existing buffers and trims itself.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2433,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2433,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ef856382_091eae2a",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/Device.h",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 609,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000032
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T01:09:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "what if the buffer manager returns to you a pair of Buffer, and the size is actually created it as. (you can also just use GetSize(), but returning the size makes it a bit more obvious you might get something not equal to what you asked for).\n\nThen, the calling code can make sure to size it as a full-size write based on the returned size.\n\nmaybe the LRU can have a parameter for maxBufferSize so it doesn\u0027t give you something too large.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 607,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 609,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "202ab32e_0016cb14",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/TempGPUBufferManager.h",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T15:24:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "While this is something we\u0027d like to have at some point, I don\u0027t know that it is a good time to do it yet, however it will be increasingly useful in the future.\n\nAt the moment we create resources during encoding. This means that we don\u0027t know when the buffer will be used and when it can be returned for reuse so this is basically a proxy in front of the backend\u0027s general purpose buffer allocators that tries to cache allocations. It seems like it should be the job of the backend allocators (or the D3D11 driver) because we don\u0027t have a lot more information than they do.\n\nIn the future when we allocate only during submission and not during encoding then we\u0027ll be able to do better than the drivers as we\u0027ll have more semantic information. We\u0027ll be able to use one or several buffers that we allocate temporary scratch space (a buffer + offset pair) out of that it returned immediately. The allocation is very short-lived so management should be simpler and result in less peak memory used.\n\nDo we have performance data that shows that this is already necessary vs. when allocation is done only during submission?",
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "620ab564_e5ed3eac",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/TempGPUBufferManager.h",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-09T16:17:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is one thing our allocator could be better than Driver. I think driver doesn\u0027t reuse allocations, when we release a buffer, its allocation is deleted immediately. However, our allocator could keep the allocations alive for some submissions, so that the number of allocations/deallocations are greatly reduced. That in turns would reduce kernel/driver calls.\n\nAlternatively, I can put this buffers reuse on hold. Instead we would never create temp buffers for D3D11, D3D11 backend already has an ability to allocate d3d11::Buffer capable of Storage + MapRead. It just needs double memory for that. And the memory used for Storage wouldn\u0027t be reused for other d3d11::Buffer. I guess it would be good enough for now.",
      "parentUuid": "202ab32e_0016cb14",
      "revId": "6dc1193f817b6b8f7ec4e73d0846a119dcf5755b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    }
